import Site from '../../layouts/Site.astro';
---
<Site
  title="Methodology"
  current="methodology"
  description="How WhiteThroated benchmarks are run and judged, and how to reproduce the pilot matrix."
>
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    How benchmarks are run and judged. Distilled from the framework's <code>BENCHMARKS.md</code>;
    fine detail lives in the pilot report and in that file.
  </p>

  <h2>Control hierarchy (always in this order)</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Same WhiteThroated path before vs after a change.</li>
    <li>Equivalent raw SwiftNIO control (transport lower bound, no framework routing).</li>
    <li>Equivalent WhiteThroated responder modes (prepared / sync / future / async).</li>
    <li>
      External frameworks (Actix Web reference, Express/Hono controls) only after Swift-side
      overhead is understood. External controls live outside the Swift repository, which stays
      Swift-only.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>External contract routes</h2>
  <p>
    Every cross-framework comparison uses the same three routes with identical semantics:
    <code>GET /</code> returns an empty 2xx response; <code>GET /user/:id</code> returns the
    route parameter as the body; <code>POST /user</code> returns an empty 2xx response. Never
    compare WhiteThroated's prepared diagnostic route against another framework's dynamic route.
  </p>

  <h2>Concurrency gates</h2>
  <p>
    Run the matrix at 64, 256, and 512 concurrent connections. All three levels are independent
    acceptance gates: a change is not a win if peak throughput rises while another level's tail
    latency regresses materially.
  </p>

  <h2>Throughput vs latency runs</h2>
  <p>
    Collect separate throughput-only samples (primary RPS result) and latency-enabled samples
    (paired distribution: min, average, p50, p75, p90, p99, p99.999, max). The Swift benchmark
    client opens all connections before the timer starts, keeps one request in flight per
    connection, and excludes connection setup from measurement. Timestamp collection is part of
    the client workload, so compared servers must use the same latency setting.
  </p>

  <h2>What every published result records</h2>
  <p>
    Requests/second; min/avg/p50/p75/p90/p99/p99.999/max latency; CPU and resident memory; Swift
    and SwiftNIO versions; external versions when applicable; OS and CPU model; exact commands;
    commit SHA; warm-up, rounds, and ordering. Raw samples are archived with the record.
  </p>

  <h2>Evidence gates (non-negotiable)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>CI smoke numbers are not performance evidence and are never published.</li>
    <li><code>COMPLETE.txt</code> means a campaign executed; it does not mean gates passed.</li>
    <li>
      Never mix runs across load-generator versions. Two published errata required full
      rebaselining: the client event-loop distribution fix and the TCP socket-option correction.
      Historical labels affected by them must carry the erratum, not silent reinterpretation.
    </li>
    <li>In-process ns/op diagnostics explain costs; they never substitute for network measurements.</li>
    <li>
      Sub-10% claims need the strengthened protocol: quiet-host preflight, rotated/alternating
      order, and enough rounds to beat run-to-run variance. Within-noise results are reported as
      neutral, never as wins.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Reproducing the pilot matrix</h2>
  <p>
    The pilot report reproduces its matrix with the server and client commands in
    <code>BENCHMARKS.md</code> — that file is part of the framework repository, not of this
    site's data pack, so the exact commands are not quoted here. The report fixes everything
    else (report, Network experiment):
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Servers: both baseline modes, current default, and raw NIO.</li>
    <li>Routes: <code>GET /</code>, <code>GET /user/42</code>, <code>POST /user</code>, plus the response-stream controls.</li>
    <li>Affinities: server CPUs 0–3 with two event-loop threads, client CPUs 4–7.</li>
    <li>Counts: 20,000-request warm-up, then separate 250,000-request throughput and latency samples per cell.</li>
    <li>Flags: HTTP/1.1 persistent connections, no TLS or compression, client override flag <code>TCP_NODELAY=0</code> (effective value unverified — see errata).</li>
    <li>Order: four rounds alternating server order, at 64, 256, and 512 connections.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <a href="/reports/tier1-default-2026-10-18/">Read the full pilot report</a> or
    <a href="/data/tier1-default-2026-10-18.json">download the raw measurement data</a>.
  </p>
</Site>
